[D,README.md,72] #### <a name="parse"></a> `> ssri.parse(integrityString) -> Integrity`
[A,README.md,73] #### <a name="parse"></a> `> ssri.parse(sri, [opts]) -> Integrity`
[C,README.md,74] 
[D,README.md,74] Parses an `integrity` string into an `Integrity` data structure. The resulting
[D,README.md,75] object has this shape:
[A,README.md,75] Parses `sri` into an `Integrity` data structure. `sri` can be an integrity
[A,README.md,76] string, an `IntegrityMetadata`-like with `digest` and `algorithm` fields and an
[A,README.md,77] optional `options` field, or an `Integrity`-like object. The resulting object
[A,README.md,78] will be an `Integrity` instance that has this shape:
[C,README.md,79] 
[C,README.md,80] ```javascript
[C,README.md,81] {
[C,README.md,82]   'sha1': [{algorithm: 'sha1', digest: 'deadbeef', options: []}],
[C,README.md,83]   'sha512': [
[C,README.md,84]     {algorithm: 'sha512', digest: 'c0ffee', options: []},
[C,README.md,85]     {algorithm: 'sha512', digest: 'bad1dea', options: ['foo']}
[C,README.md,86]   ],
[C,README.md,87] }
[C,README.md,88] ```
[C,README.md,89] 
[A,README.md,90] If `opts.strict` is truthy, the resulting object will be filtered such that
[A,README.md,91] it strictly follows the Subresource Integrity spec, throwing away any entries
[A,README.md,92] with any invalid components. This also means a restricted set of algorithms
[A,README.md,93] will be used -- the spec limits them to `sha256`, `sha384`, and `sha512`.
[A,README.md,94] 
[A,README.md,95] Strict mode is recommended if the integrity strings are intended for use in
[A,README.md,96] browsers, or in other situations where strict adherence to the spec is needed.
[A,README.md,97] 
[C,README.md,98] ##### Example
[C,README.md,99] 
[C,README.md,100] ```javascript
[C,README.md,101] ssri.parse('sha512-9KhgCRIx/AmzC8xqYJTZRrnO8OW2Pxyl2DIMZSBOr0oDvtEFyht3xpp71j/r/pAe1DM+JI/A+line3jUBgzQ7A==?foo') // -> Integrity
[C,README.md,102] ```
[C,README.md,103] 
[D,README.md,93] #### <a name="integrity-concat"></a> `> Integrity#concat(otherIntegrity) -> Integrity`
[A,README.md,104] #### <a name="integrity-concat"></a> `> Integrity#concat(otherIntegrity, [opts]) -> Integrity`
[C,README.md,105] 
[C,README.md,106] Concatenates an `Integrity` object with another IntegrityLike, or a string
[C,README.md,107] representing integrity metadata.
[C,README.md,108] 
[C,README.md,109] This is functionally equivalent to concatenating the string format of both
[C,README.md,110] integrity arguments, and calling [`ssri.parse`](#ssri-parse) on the new string.
[C,README.md,111] 
[A,README.md,112] If `opts.strict` is true, the new `Integrity` will be created using strict
[A,README.md,113] parsing rules. See [`ssri.parse`](#parse).
[A,README.md,114] 
[C,README.md,115] ##### Example
[C,README.md,116] 
[C,README.md,117] ```javascript
[C,README.md,118] // This will combine the integrity checks for two different versions of
[C,README.md,119] // your index.js file so you can use a single integrity string and serve
[C,README.md,120] // either of these to clients, from a single `<script>` tag.
[C,README.md,121] const desktopIntegrity = ssri.fromData(fs.readFileSync('./index.desktop.js'))
[C,README.md,122] const mobileIntegrity = ssri.fromData(fs.readFileSync('./index.mobile.js'))
[C,README.md,123] 
[C,README.md,124] // Note that browsers (and ssri) will succeed as long as ONE of the entries
[C,README.md,125] // for the *prioritized* algorithm succeeds. That is, in order for this fallback
[C,README.md,126] // to work, both desktop and mobile *must* use the same `algorithm` values.
[C,README.md,127] desktopIntegrity.concat(mobileIntegrity)
[C,README.md,128] ```
[C,README.md,129] 
